      Severity:  2.3 (low)
      Solution:  Upgrade to >=0.23.46

      Crate:     rustls
      Version:   0.23.45
      Title:     Brotli certificate decompression can allocate up to 1 GiB before authentication
      Date:      2026-10-14
      ID:        RUSTSEC-2026-0348
      URL:       https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0348
      Severity:  3.7 (low)
      Solution:  Upgrade to >=0.23.46

      Crate:     rustls-webpki
      Version:   0.103.15
      Title:     Certificate verification may accept distrusted intermediates
      Date:      2026-10-20
      ID:        RUSTSEC-2026-0382
      URL:       https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0382
      Solution:  Upgrade to >=0.103.16, <0.104.0-alpha.1 OR >=0.104.0-alpha.8

      Crate:     webpki-roots
      Version:   1.0.9
      Title:     Bundled root certificates allow trust in distrusted emSign intermediates
      Date:      2026-10-19
      ID:        RUSTSEC-2026-0381
      URL:       https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0381
      Solution:  Upgrade to >=1.0.10
  - Fix bug: Honor --dry-run in frozen, unfrozen, pin and unpin
  - Shorten subcommand help to one line each; add a footer pointing to `<command> --help`:
    - slacker --help now looks much prettier
    - slacker <CMD> -h is very short
    - slacker <CMD> --help full detailed
  - Limit dependency recursion depth to prevent stack overflow from a crafted repo:
    - We did a reverse engineering of the binaries and then a full source audit. At the critical points (GPG pinning, verify, path-traversal guards argv, SQL, downloads, credentials) slacker is clean, no RCE/privesc.
      We found a real pre-auth DoS: unbounded recursion in add_with_deps, which with a malicious dependency chain dropped the stack. We reproduced it end-to-end.
      The fix: 7 lines only in main.rs, MAX_DEP_DEPTH = 512. It measures depth, not count, so it doesn't touch legitimate installations. 219 tests pass.
      What the attacker needs: to check the metadata of a repo that the victim has in the repos. That is, one of the repositories has been hacked or a future repository has to be "smartly" malicious.
      Three realistic ways:
      Malicious third-party repo. Someone creates a tempting repo (a build of some popular package that is missing from SBo), people add it with add-repo. From there, they have full control over the PACKAGES.TXT they serve.
      Compromised or malicious mirror of a repo you already trust.
      MITM on http repo. If the repo is http:// (or https that is broken), the attacker on the network changes the PACKAGES.TXT in-stream.
      The attack, step by step:
      In PACKAGES.TXT (or in per-package .dep files) they put a linear chain of dependencies: pkgA requires pkgB, pkgB requires pkgC, and so on, with ~10-20 thousand different names. It is a text file of a few MB, trivial to create.
      The victim does slacker update (downloads metadata) and then anything that touches the dependency resolution, even harmless ones: slacker install pkgA, or just slacker upgrade-all, or even --dry-run.
      add_with_deps calls itself once per link in the chain. At ~8-10 thousand links, the 8 MB stack of the main thread is exhausted and slacker dies with SIGABRT: stack overflow.
      Why it was more insidious than it seems: the crash happened in the design phase, before any package was even downloaded or verified. That is, the whole GPG trust model, the pinned keys, the checksums, did not protect you at all, because the signature check comes later. And the metadata itself (PACKAGES.TXT, .dep) is not GPG-signed, only CHECKSUMS is. So it was pre-authentication: you would crash with input that doesn't pass any signature.
      What the attacker gains: denial of service. Slacker becomes useless as long as the malicious repo is active, every time you run update/install/upgrade it crashes.
      It's NOT RCE: Rust on stack overflow does a controlled abort with guard page, not memory corruption, so NO attacker code is executed. But in a root tool that is the only way to keep the system up to date, being able to reliably drop a repo is a real problem.
      After the fix, at link 512 it stops cleanly with a dependency chain too deep message, without touching anything.
      - After Cap dependency recursion depth also quarantine repos that exceed it

